Z||000004||theogre||11-25-2001||05:11 PM||||Ebay has a long history of doing little or nothing about fraud on their system. They've been involved repeatedly with state and federal authorities over it. They've so far done nothing but pay lip service to the FTC over repeated problems with auctions.<P>If you read the terms Ebay operates under, they basically say screw you, and once their "arbitrator" rules agains you, screw you some more. These terms are precisly why I'll never do businness on their service.<P>The only hope you have is if you paid via credit card. Use the credit card companies complaint system and have it charged back. Many, if not all, cards have provisions for protection against internet and other fraud and all sorts of customer satisfaction problem.<P>As fast as Ebay does cancel a sellers acount, the seller just creates a new account. Ebay and the others make real no effort to prevent fraud and rarely do anything to prosecute Fraudulent sellers. Only when State or FBI shows up at Ebay with a court order does a scamming dealer ever see any real penalty.<P>Those of you who've been screwed by Outlaw or whatever clown is behind that need to stop whining and file complaints with your state attourny generals office and maybe the FBI's internet fruad department. You probably won't get your money but you may be able to get him jailed or severely inconvienenced. ||||reg||1
